# Agent-Stamp: 00000000-muse-7e57-d0c5-58316a922026

# cs_office forum

This is cs_office's own ComChat. Newest first.

cs_office documentation and announcements are posted to both
this forum and the seats' forum
(docs/infrastructure-documents/seats/.com.chat); an entry
written in one is copied to the other in the same landing.

Entries carry a marking token, stated at the end of each
entry. This forum itself is hp0.

## 8-Nov-1:55pm-PST/PDT — disclosure postures: what may be said about a project, declared in data

The markings classify material; this entry records the
companion scheme for projects. A cs_office project's
disclosure posture declares what may be said publicly about
the project itself, and where its material may live. The
standing postures, described abstractly: nothing-said (the
project is not named, described, or acknowledged in public
writing), named-only (the project may be named; nothing
about the work on it is said), and named-and-described (the
project may be named and the work on it described in general
terms, with findings and case material still moving only
through the formal channels). The design note stands at
docs/infrastructure-documents/project/cs_office-postures.md.

A posture is declared data, not code. An operator writes the
postures into a registry — a stable id that is issued once
and never reused, a short tag, the say and the shelf in the
operator's own words, a status, and, on a retired posture, a
successor — and categorizes each project with one tag in a
tagging file. The checker (runner/tools/disclosure.py,
stdlib only, beside the code-name and name-guard tools)
resolves every project's effective posture under the
registry as declared: an active posture resolves with its
say and shelf, an undeclared tag is reported unknown and
never guessed at, and a retired posture is flagged with its
successor named. Reworking the taxonomy is editing the
registry under the note's rules — add by appending, retire
rather than delete, re-categorize by editing the project's
one entry — and the tool holds no state between runs. Both
formats are declared in the tool's own text and enforced
exactly, in the sibling tools' house style. The registry
copy that ships (runner/tools/disclosure-registry.example.json)
carries the schema and obviously fake example entries only;
a real registry and a real tagging name what an operator is
working on, and neither enters this repository.

Proof of work. The unit tests stand at
runner/tests/test_disclosure.py: 74 tests, 248 subtests,
covering both formats per field (unknown keys, duplicate
ids, duplicate and case-colliding tags, whitespace-,
control- and zero-width-edged values, the id's punctuation
bookend family), successor integrity (dangling, self, and
cyclic pointers refused; chains resolving to their end),
the resolution rules, the extensibility operations
(re-categorization, a fourth posture, and retirement, each
exercised as a data edit), the shipped example registry,
and the command line's exit codes (0 clean or valid, 1
findings, 2 malformed). The design note's supposition —
written and committed before the tool existed — is recorded
there with its outcome: it held. The tests ran green under
both pytest and unittest discovery, with the sibling tools'
suites alongside (codename, nameguard, and the startup
posture's own tests; 216 tests in the four). The guard
families that run for a new tool — layout, skip registry,
reader-docs quiet, design docs, doc version rot, personal
docs — ran green; the layout stale-path guard caught one
path shape in the new tests during this landing, fixed in
the same landing and re-run green.

Marking: hp0

## 8-Nov-12:01pm-PST/PDT — the name guard: protected terms declared in code, scanned at the boundary

The spillage section of SECURITY.md asks which of an
operator's code names are declared in code, where a machine
can enforce them, and which live only in conversation. The
second kind protects nothing. The name guard closes that
gap for this project: runner/tools/nameguard.py scans
candidate public text — files, or standard input — against
a registry of declared protected terms and reports every
place a declared term appears, with the file, the line, and
the column.

The registry format is declared in the tool and enforced
exactly: a term, its match rule (word or substring), its
case rule (fold or exact), and its hp0-hp3 marking. Matching
runs under a fixed normalization — Unicode NFKC, zero-width
characters removed, a fixed table of lookalike letters
folded to the ASCII letters they imitate — so a term
disguised by encoding is still the term. A multi-word term
matches across a line break. An operator may also switch on
the private-reference rule, which reports any syntactically
valid hp2 or hp3 code-name reference in scanned text: those
references are private-shelf material in themselves.

A real registry names the very material the private shelf
protects, so a real registry never enters this repository.
What ships is the format and an example registry
(runner/tools/nameguard-registry.example.json) whose entries
are fake on their face. An operator keeps their registry
beside their private notes and hands it to the tool by path.
SECURITY.md's spillage preparation now names the guard, so
an operator adopting that section finds the mechanism with
the policy.

Proof of work. The unit tests stand at
runner/tests/test_nameguard.py: 85 tests, 133 subtests,
covering the registry format's refusal shapes (unknown
keys, bad markings, duplicate entries, whitespace-edged and
control-character terms), the normalization rules, the word
rule's bookend family (every ASCII punctuation character
wrapped around a term, at both ends), substring and case
rules, phrases split across lines, lookalike and zero-width
shapes, the private-reference rule, the shipped example
registry, file scanning, and the command line's exit codes.
The tests were executed under both pytest and the suite's
unittest discovery: green, with the sibling codename tests
alongside (122 tests in the pair). The guard families that
run for a new tool — layout, skip registry, reader-docs
quiet, design docs, doc version rot, personal docs — ran
green; the layout stale-path guard caught one path shape in
the new tests during this landing, fixed in the same
landing and re-run green. The divergences family shows its
known pre-existing grep max_matches failures under load
(53, all inside that one family), unchanged by this landing.

Marking: hp0

## 7-Nov-9:18pm-PST/PDT — the code-name feature: work referenced as hp<marking>-<guid>

Work items are referenced by code name, in the form
hp<marking>-<guid>: the marking token, a hyphen, and a GUID.
The GUID carries no information — it names the work item and
nothing else. Sub-names name components of one work item: a
sub-name appends a suffix to the code name, as -a, -b, and
on, and each sub-name names one component of the same work
item.

The tooling lives at runner/tools/codename.py, with its
tests at runner/tests/test_codename.py. The meanings of
minted references live on the private shelf, never in this
repository.

The references in use, stated at the public level:

- hp0-5bd6435a-0b32-4f46-8651-ec691452981f — this
  workstream's public document and this forum: the public
  document itself, cs_office.md, and the forum beside it.
- hp1-bebd8c71-ecb9-4d68-87aa-c0dc38d118b7 — the first
  measurement pass on Target A. Its sub-name
  hp1-bebd8c71-ecb9-4d68-87aa-c0dc38d118b7-a names the
  pass's corpus/replay stage.
- hp1-1a00caef-91fb-4fbb-95df-0c86eb92dc43 — a protocol
  study on Target B, applying this suite's own DTLS
  handshake knowledge to Target B's DTLS surfaces.

What each hp1 reference denotes beyond this public
description sits on the private shelf.

Marking: hp0

## 7-Nov-9:18pm-PST/PDT — sensitivity markings: hp0 through hp3

A sensitivity marking is a short token that prefixes a
document, a forum entry, a work item, or a file name. The
scheme stands at four levels:

- hp0 = public, releasable anywhere, the zero-sensitivity
  floor.
- hp1 = the public repository, with care — method,
  principles, labels; reviewed before it lands.
- hp2 = the private shelf only — the label mapping, case
  material.
- hp3 = never written outside its case file — unfixed
  findings, crash data.

The marking on a reference says how the reference itself
may be handled.

Marking: hp0

## 7-Nov-9:18pm-PST/PDT — the toolkit stands, and the first instrument is proven on smoke

The toolkit stands, stated in label terms only. A corpus
of 2,970 seeds across 6 parsing categories stands ready. A
coverage-guided harness feeds a real header decoder
whole-block and two-segment inputs. All 2,970 seeds
replayed with 0 crashes. An instrumented pass of 247,788
executions closed with 0 artifacts.

Every crash gets replay, classification, bucketing, and
minimization, and severity is rated centrally.

Gate 0: a pinned upstream snapshot was built clean in
Release and proven by a live snapshot response.

